  <npc_parties>
    <overview>
      <rule>Wandering monster results may include NPC adventuring parties.</rule>
      <rule>NPC parties can be generated by procedure or created ad hoc as needed.</rule>
    </overview>

    <generation_procedure>
      <step order="1">Roll 1d4+2 to determine the number encountered.</step>
      <step order="2">For each NPC, roll 3d6 on the NPC Class table to determine class.</step>
      <step order="3">Roll 1d6 once to determine party alignment.</step>
      <step order="4">All NPCs are either of that alignment or within one step of it.</step>
      <step order="5">Determine the party's base level.</step>
      <step order="6">Roll 1d6 on the NPC Level table to determine each NPC's actual level from the base level.</step>
      <step order="7">Optionally replace one or more NPCs with 2 NPCs of the same class but two levels lower; these are henchmen of the higher-level characters present.</step>
      <step order="8">Equip NPCs with mundane weapons, armor, and equipment appropriate to class, level, and terrain.</step>
      <step order="9">If in the wilderness, each NPC has a 10% chance per level of having a mount.</step>
      <step order="10">Determine treasure carried by each NPC using the Treasure Type by Level table.</step>
      <step order="11">Do not roll magic items from the Treasure Type table.</step>
      <step order="12">Instead, for each category of magic item the NPC can use, there is a 5% chance per NPC level that the NPC possesses a useful item of that category.</step>
    </generation_procedure>

    <base_level_rules>
      <rule>Base level is the party's average level.</rule>
      <rule>In a dungeon, base level is determined by the dungeon level encountered, as shown on the Random Monsters by Level tables.</rule>
      <rule>In the wilderness, base level is based on the maximum level of the nearest dungeon.</rule>
      <rule>In a settlement, base level is 7 minus the settlement's market class.</rule>
    </base_level_rules>

    <alignment_rules>
      <rule>Roll 1d6 to determine party alignment.</rule>
      <rule>All NPCs are either of the rolled alignment or one step away from it.</rule>
    </alignment_rules>

